{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"b977b1ddc3c78355","name":"card_event_forward_observers","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Britain/Homefront/events/card_event_forward_observers","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"FORWARD OBSERVERS","text":"Deal 2 damage to all enemy units. If you deployed a British infantry last turn, draw a card.","flavorText":"During WWII, Forward Observation Officers (FOOs) became vital to British artillery operations. As indirect fire gradually allowed for increased engagement distances, FOOs acted as the eyes of their batteries, embedded with infantry or tank units at the front. Positioned in forward posts or in armored carriers or tanks like the Stuart or Sherman, they identified targets, coordinated fire missions, and communicated directly with their artillery units. FOOs had the authority to call in fire from their own battery, and from mid-war, some could command fire from their entire regiment. Select officers designated as Commander’s Representatives could even access divisional or corps artillery. This decentralized control allowed for rapid, localized responses in fast-moving operations. Often attached to a company or squadron, FOOs developed quick fire plans involving multiple coordinated targets to support limited offensives. These plans were crucial in assaults and defensive actions, blending artillery firepower with frontline maneuvering. By integrating with supported units and adapting to evolving battlefield demands, FOOs played a central role in the success of British artillery throughout the war.","faction":"Britain","type":"order","rarity":"Rare","cost":4,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_forward_observers","title":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_event_forward_observers_title","SourceString":"FORWARD OBSERVERS","LocalizedString":"FORWARD OBSERVERS"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Britain","kredits":4,"Text":{"Na